<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dordrecht</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dort</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dordrecht is a city in South Holland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, where several branches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Maas</a> delta meet. Granted town rights in 1220, it is the oldest chartered town of Holland and long held staple rights over river trade. The first free assembly of the States of Holland met there in 1572 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synod-of-dort">Synod of Dort</a> of 1618–1619 defined Reformed orthodoxy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dordrecht.jpg" alt="Dordrecht" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ben Bender · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augustijnenkamp,_3311_Dordrecht,_Netherlands_-_panoramio_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
